+#+TITLE: /spec-report report
+
+* status
+Not implemented. The spec exists, but the skill does not, and no
+behavior in the repo contradicts the spec. It is not ready to
+implement yet. Most of the spec is concrete, but it leaves open how a
+=spec--*= spec maps to its report name and how a spec marks a
+conflict resolution plan.
+
+This report was written by hand against the spec, because the skill
+it describes does not exist yet.
+
+* conflicts
+None. Nothing implements the spec, so nothing can contradict it.
+
+* conflict resolution plans that can be removed
+None. The spec contains no conflict resolution plans.
+
+* not implemented
+** the skill itself (spec.org:3, spec.org:5-33)
+Not ready to implement, because of the questions below. Everything
+else is concrete enough to plan.
+
+Evidence:
+- =.claude/skills/spec-report/= holds only =spec.org=; there is no
+  =SKILL.md=. The sibling skills =freebsd-port=, =patch-triage=, and
+  =process-email= each have one.
+- Invoking =/spec-report= in a session reports that no such command
+  is available.
+- =jj log -r 'files(".claude/skills/spec-report")'= shows a single
+  commit, 3d0fecae "claude/skills/spec-report: Initial spec".
+
+The existing work gives a pattern to follow:
+- =freebsd-port= pairs =spec.org= with a =SKILL.md= that says
+  "spec.org beside this file is the specification; this file is how
+  to carry it out" (=.claude/skills/freebsd-port/SKILL.md:26-27=).
+- =src/sclr/agent.org= already defines a =report.org= written next to
+  the spec, and the "Own the loop" section of =CLAUDE.md= names
+  "report.org exists" as a trustworthy completion signal.
+
+Questions for the spec:
+1. What is the report name for =spec--NAME.org= and =SPEC--NAME.md=?
+   Is it =report--NAME.org= and =REPORT--NAME.md=? spec.org:15 gives
+   only =report.org= and =REPORT.md= as examples, and two specs in one
+   directory would otherwise write the same report.
+2. How does a spec mark a conflict resolution plan, so the skill can
+   say whether one exists for a conflict (spec.org:19-22)? No spec in
+   the repo has such a section to copy. For example, is it a heading
+   such as =* conflicts= with one entry per conflict?
+3. What counts as "notes" as opposed to implementation and
+   documentation (spec.org:11-12)? Candidates in the repo include
+   =zib/=, =research.org=, =lore.org=, =collab.org=, and =todo.org=.
+4. Which tree is the implementation measured against: trunk, or the
+   working copy? The working copy here sits on =b/dev=, Pat's
+   mega-merge. The spec commit itself (3d0fecae) is on =b/dev= but not
+   on trunk, so the two answers can differ.
+5. Should the skill commit the report, and should it overwrite an
+   existing one? =CLAUDE.md= says to commit after successful work.
+   =src/sclr/agent.org= commits =report.org= alongside the work.
+6. =src/sclr= also writes =report.org= next to a spec, but with a
+   different shape (an account of the result rather than a gap
+   analysis). May both tools write to the same file, or should one of
+   them use another name?
+7. Should =disable-model-invocation= be set, as for =freebsd-port=?
+   Or may the model run the skill on its own?
+
+** the report as an artifact (spec.org:3)
+Not produced by the skill. This file follows the spec's report shape,
+but it was written by hand, so it does not show that the skill works.
+
+* implemented
+** the spec
+=.claude/skills/spec-report/spec.org= exists, added in 3d0fecae
+"claude/skills/spec-report: Initial spec". Nothing else in the spec is
+implemented.

